\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda|u|^{2^*(\beta)-2}u^2,\quad\mbox{in}\;\;\mathbb{R}^4,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{split} \end{align*} where $2<\beta<4$ and $|u|^{2^*(\beta)-2}u^2$ represents a critical nonhomogeneous growth term with respect to the given conformal variation. We establish a standardized ground state condition and prove the existence of a ground state solution meeting this condition, by employing a homotopy-based minimax principle and a compact embedding theorem. Additionally, we analyze the behavior of the ground state energy and obtain an estimate of the normalized ground state solution. This work extends earlier research by N. Shakerouk and S.J. Li, providing a broader analysis of the biharmonic Choquard type equation with critical growth terms. This extension is notable as it considers a type of critical nonhomogeneous term that has not been previously investigated. This term represents a critical variation of the original Choquard type equation, with an exponential growth term that is crucial in defining the embedding of the Sobolev space. This new term raises interesting questions about the compactness of the Sobolev embedding, as it induces a type of resonance that occurs at the same frequency as the maximum of the operator. This resonance plays a vital role in defining the behavior of the ground state solution, and is a key factor in proving the existence of a standardized ground state solution. This proof relies on a detailed analysis of the operator's resolvent, as well as a comparison of the Sobolev embedding and compactness theorems. This analysis extends earlier research by Li and Li, providing a more comprehensive understanding of the biharmonic Choquard type equation with critical growth terms. This new approach to solving the equation is a key result, as it proves the existence of a standardized ground state solution in the case where the critical nonhomogeneous term represents a critical variation of the original Choquard type equation. This solution plays a vital role in defining the behavior of the ground state energy, and provides a more 